--TEST--
`Core\Signal::onShutdown` registers a handler, replaces it when called again, and runs neither while nothing has asked this process to stop — `rule:core-api/tier-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The class is a registration and nothing else: what runs the callable is a
// delivered terminating signal reaching the request's next safepoint, and no
// signal has been delivered to this program. So the boundary worth pinning is
// the one a registration may not cross — handing the callable over is not
// allowed to call it, and a class that ran it to "check" it prints a line here
// the expectation does not have.
Core\Signal::onShutdown(fn (): void => {
    echo "first ran\n";
});
echo "registered\n";

// One slot rather than a queue, exactly as the two `Core\Fatal` handlers are:
// a second registration replaces the first. That is asserted here by the same
// silence, because a queue and a replacement are only told apart when a
// shutdown arrives and neither may run now.
Core\Signal::onShutdown(fn (): void => {
    echo "second ran\n";
});
echo "replaced\n";

// The statements after a registration are the ordinary ones. Every safepoint
// this loop crosses is a place the handler *would* run had a signal arrived,
// so a class that raised its own shutdown flag on registration would print
// inside this loop rather than after it.
var $total = 0;
for (var $i = 0; $i < 3; $i = $i + 1) {
    $total = $total + $i;
}
echo "ran on: ", $total, "\n";
--EXPECT--
registered
replaced
ran on: 3
